1
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:14,600
You okay? Oh, I'm fine.

2
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:16,320
It's just a house.

3
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:20,880
Yeah, just the house that you lived in for the first 18 years of your life.

4
00:00:20,940 --> 00:00:23,180
A house that you haven't even seen since your dad died.

5
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:25,940
Like I said, it's just a house.

6
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:30,700
I'm sorry. Oh, my God.

7
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:32,120
It's Daddy's chair.

8
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:35,380
The very one he was sitting in when I told him you and I were getting married.

9
00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:37,380
He got so mad.

10
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:40,840
Oh, yeah. Guess he never did get that fixed.

11
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:43,380
Okay, come here.

12
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:47,540
And look, this is where we used to play Cowboys and Indians.

13
00:00:48,060 --> 00:00:52,640
I was the Indian maiden, beautiful yet entirely self -reliant.

14
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:54,540
Yeah, still remember your Indian name.

15
00:00:54,820 --> 00:00:55,820
Abandoned by tribe.

16
00:00:58,060 --> 00:01:01,240
Thanks for reminding us, sleeps with a doll.

17
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:04,280
This is how I remember Gramps.

18
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:07,400
With the twinkling eyes and the cocky grin.

19
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:09,860
Veined, smug, self -satisfied.

20
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:12,280
I miss him.

21
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:15,620
I love this house.

22
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:18,340
Me too. Makes me feel like a kid again.

23
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:21,080
Don't you guys get too attached.

24
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:24,220
Remember, we're here to help Grandma fix this house up so she can sell it.

25
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:26,120
But why does Grandma have to move?

26
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:27,920
Well, Florida's warmer.

27
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:30,280
Besides, this house is too big for one person.

28
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:32,000
Carol could come live with Grandma.

29
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:35,740
Then, Grandma just lost her husband.

30
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:36,840
Hasn't she been through enough?

31
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:41,340
By the way, where is Grandma?

32
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:42,920
Didn't she come in?

33
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:49,080
Mom, are you coming in?

34
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:52,980
I should have stayed in Akron with your Aunt Louise.

35
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:56,360
She didn't ask me to make tough decisions.

36
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:58,000
Come on, Mom.

37
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:01,520
I know you haven't been here since the funeral, but we need you to help sort things

38
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:06,120
out. I almost expect to see your father come through that door.

39
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:10,300
There are just so many ghosts here.

40
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:14,640
Ghosts? Honey, it's just a figure of speech.

41
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:16,580
There's no such thing as ghosts.

42
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:22,360
Who let you know, shrink man?

43
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:56,260
As long as we got each other We got the world spinning right in our hands Baby, you

44
00:02:56,260 --> 00:03:07,000
and me We gotta be The luckiest dreamers who never quit dreaming As long as we keep

45
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:17,720
on giving We could take anything that comes our way Living rain or shine, all the

46
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:27,960
time We got each other sharing the laughter and love

47
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:35,680
Oh, Katie, my girl, you look even more lovely than I remembered.

48
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:37,960
But I just got one thing to say.

49
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:40,420
Have you lost your cotton -picking mind?

50
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:46,640
I mean, I heard there was trouble here, but selling our house in some 40 -odd years

51
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:52,740
of memory? I'm talking to a woman who's grinding instant coffee.

52
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:57,020
Okay, everybody, listen up.

53
00:03:57,580 --> 00:04:01,000
Tonight, Grandma Kate sleeps in her room, your dad and I have the guest room, and

54
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:02,260
the rest of you sleep down here.

55
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:05,580
Here, you expect six people to share a fold -out couch?

56
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:08,760
Hey, the floor's fine with me, so I can't pan out.

57
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:10,120
Only it's in.

58
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:14,060
Who's the towhead here?

59
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:16,860
Well, could somebody at least tell me his name?

60
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:18,300
I agree with Luke.

61
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:19,620
Oh, thank you very much.

62
00:04:20,460 --> 00:04:24,460
I can sleep anywhere as long as it's not within smelling distance of Ben's feet.

63
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:27,840
I guess Carol's sleeping outside.

64
00:04:29,320 --> 00:04:32,220
Someone's welcome to sleep in that big bed with me.

65
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:33,740
Even you, Ben.

66
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:36,880
Nothing could be worse than your grandfather's feet.

67
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:40,740
Hey, I was a cop walking an eight -hour beat.

68
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:42,220
What do you expect, rose petals?

69
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:45,700
Thanks for the offer, Mom, but you need some privacy.

70
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:46,860
This is your house.

71
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,120
Hey, Avalista, repairs we have to do before we can sell this house.

72
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:52,100
Who died and put you in charge?

73
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:55,140
Oh, I guess I did.

74
00:04:56,980 --> 00:05:00,800
For this, you called me out of a golf game with Jane Mansfield and Sophocles?

75
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:04,300
Now, why don't I fix lunch for everybody?

76
00:05:05,540 --> 00:05:08,340
The Human Granola Bar is cooking lunch.

77
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,220
I better take over.

78
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:12,680
Carol's cooking lunch?

79
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:14,600
Come on, Chrissy, let's go call for pizza.

80
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:21,620
Benny, you wanna earn a couple of dollars helping your old grandma carry some boxes?

81
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:25,900
Sure. But of course I'd rather carry them for free.

82
00:05:28,020 --> 00:05:30,040
Carol is such a nice person.

83
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:34,920
She comes into the kitchen, says, I'm a guest and she refused to let me cook lunch.

84
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:38,220
Well, it's times like this when I love her too.

85
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:42,580
Hey, Mike, I could use your help cleaning out those rain gutters.

86
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:44,160
Oh, uh, sure, Dad.

87
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:48,040
Oh! Mike, are you okay?

88
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:51,000
What happened? It's my ankle.

89
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:52,840
I twisted it.

90
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:57,280
I don't know if I can handle those gutters, Dad.

91
00:05:57,740 --> 00:05:59,420
Really? Isn't that convenient?

92
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:02,860
Oh, you cold -hearted quack.

93
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:05,620
Dr. Siever, your son is hurt.

94
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:07,600
Uh, no, no, no, no.

95
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:09,340
It's, uh, it's nothing I can't walk off.

96
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:11,460
All right, let me have that ladder.

97
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:12,900
All right, all right.

98
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:14,080
Maybe I was hasty, Mike.

99
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:16,000
Maybe Kate should take you to get that X -ray.

100
00:06:17,020 --> 00:06:18,880
Well, all right, if you say so.

101
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:22,520
Just that you've pulled so many scams in the past, I...

102
00:06:23,220 --> 00:06:28,880
I'm sorry. Okay.

103
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:31,100
Why, you little scamp!

104
00:06:31,540 --> 00:06:32,380
What are you doing?

105
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:35,340
I am getting us a day of sightseeing in Boston.

106
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:36,520
You're not hurt?

107
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:38,640
Well, if I was, do you think I could do this?

108
00:06:40,920 --> 00:06:42,220
I taught him that.

109
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:45,060
Mike Siever, you scared me half to death!

110
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:47,200
I know, I know, Kate, but I had to.

111
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:49,620
I mean, I couldn't pull off this lame scam by myself.

112
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:51,280
Oh, so you made me your patsy.

113
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:55,020
Well, no, technically, Dad was the patsy, and you were my stooge.

114
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:57,480
When are you gonna grow up?

115
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,100
Women. Sheesh! I got it.

116
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:05,500
You were going to the emergency room.

117
00:07:05,980 --> 00:07:08,880
Uh, Dad, look, I don't care how bad the pain is.

118
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:10,320
I'm gonna help you with those gutters.

119
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:12,200
Oh, I see.

120
00:07:12,420 --> 00:07:13,800
Kate didn't want to go sightseeing.

121
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:23,640
Gosh, this box is heavy.

122
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:27,920
Here, Ben, don't tell your mother.

123
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:30,880
Mom, I'm standing right here.

124
00:07:31,320 --> 00:07:36,420
Big mouth. Mom, you are on a fixed income.

125
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:38,720
You can't keep giving your money away to an extortionist.

126
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:41,220
I didn't know Ben was double -jointed.

127
00:07:42,820 --> 00:07:44,300
Oh, let's get to it.

128
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:46,120
Only so much will fit in your condo.

129
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:47,820
Oh, I'm awful at this.

130
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:50,940
I never know what to save and what to throw away.

131
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:53,380
Well, here's something you can throw away.

132
00:07:53,380 --> 00:07:54,540
Your old -fashioned juicer.

133
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:57,580
Oh, no. I'm going to Florida.

134
00:07:57,700 --> 00:07:58,880
I'll need that.

135
00:07:59,220 --> 00:08:01,240
Your mom's got an electric juicer downstairs.

136
00:08:01,420 --> 00:08:02,700
I never use that one.

137
00:08:02,740 --> 00:08:05,180
Why not? I hate orange juice.

138
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:09,840
See what I put up with for 46 years?

139
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:12,040
I brought some boxes.

140
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:15,000
Oh! The Enchanted Castle!

141
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:18,680
What do you know, my old WW2 puff tent?

142
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:23,420
Grandpa and I used to play this game where I was the princess and Grandpa was the

143
00:08:23,420 --> 00:08:27,700
gallant knight who would have to slay the dragon with the horrible breath.

144
00:08:28,060 --> 00:08:29,400
Otherwise known as Mike.

145
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:34,440
Oh, look! There's Jason.

146
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:37,840
Yoo -hoo! Jason, how are the gutters coming?

147
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,040
Look, if you fall, try to land on your hair.

148
00:08:49,020 --> 00:08:52,260
All right, Mike, don't turn that water on until I've got a...

149
00:08:52,260 --> 00:08:57,980
Whoa! Whoa! Look at that kid run.

150
00:09:00,780 --> 00:09:04,960
Margaret, I'm sorry that I didn't take you on that fishing trip that I promised to.

151
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:08,760
And I know you can't hear me when I'm talking to you, but you see, I got this long

152
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:13,220
checklist to things I'm supposed to apologize for and...

153
00:09:13,220 --> 00:09:19,020
Oh. Well, that's two down and 3,163 to go.

154
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:22,660
Listen, I've got some apologies to make to you two.

155
00:09:22,760 --> 00:09:26,560
Like that time at your wedding when I called you a bustering spinach for brains

156
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:29,160
nincompoop. I'm sorry.

157
00:09:29,980 --> 00:09:30,860
No, I'm not.

158
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:31,600
Who wrote this?

159
00:09:32,780 --> 00:09:35,380
I'm gonna go knock that window screen back into place.

160
00:09:36,420 --> 00:09:40,120
Oh, wow. Look at all these letters that Grandma saved from Grandpa.

161
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:42,220
Yeah. Woman doesn't throw out anything.

162
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:47,440
Who's that? Your father.

163
00:09:49,740 --> 00:09:51,500
Oh, wow. This is incredible.

164
00:09:51,660 --> 00:09:54,860
What? Oh, don't read that.

165
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:58,400
That's private. My dearest Katie.

166
00:09:58,640 --> 00:09:59,560
Oh, young people.

167
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:00,740
They never listen.

168
00:10:03,380 --> 00:10:06,800
Jason, shouldn't we hire someone to make these repairs?

169
00:10:07,320 --> 00:10:08,780
It wasn't my fault.

170
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:10,540
I was swinging the hammer.

171
00:10:10,720 --> 00:10:11,720
I inhaled a moth.

172
00:10:13,260 --> 00:10:16,560
Mike, go upstairs and get a Band -Aid and some antiseptic.

173
00:10:16,820 --> 00:10:19,500
And maylocks. Sure.

174
00:10:22,820 --> 00:10:25,960
Oh, Jason. Oh.

175
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:31,440
Now that I have you alone, I'm so worried about my mother.

176
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:35,980
She seems vague, indecisive, confused.

177
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:37,700
Totally out of touch with reality?

178
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:39,400
You've noticed it too?

179
00:10:39,860 --> 00:10:45,300
Since 1968. No, Jason, this is worse than usual.

180
00:10:45,960 --> 00:10:47,680
Honey, she's grieving over Ed.

181
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:48,700
You've got to bear with her.

182
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:52,440
I'm trying, but she is really getting to me.

183
00:10:53,420 --> 00:10:54,980
Thank God she's moving to Florida.

184
00:10:54,980 --> 00:10:57,600
It's obvious she can't handle this house by herself.

185
00:10:57,680 --> 00:10:59,620
Oh, it's not as bad as you think.

186
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:02,500
Mommy, Daddy, come quick.

187
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:05,360
Grandma got her hair stuck in the sofa bed.

188
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:27,080
Oh, Jenny. Oh, Jenny.

189
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:34,200
What made me think I'd be able to sleep with this rabble?

190
00:11:35,680 --> 00:11:38,380
I know. I'll take Grandma up on our offer.

191
00:11:42,740 --> 00:12:04,160
Grandma? Oh, my darling Katie, just look at you.

192
00:12:04,160 --> 00:12:06,180
They're still sleeping on your side of the bed.

193
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:08,300
You can spread out now if you want to,

194
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:12,660
you know. Ah, Katie, what are you gonna do without me?

195
00:12:14,060 --> 00:12:18,040
The woman who spent 20 minutes at a photo -mat trying to buy a bucket of chicken.

196
00:12:19,380 --> 00:12:23,740
The woman who tried to cash one of those million dollar Ed McMahon checks.

197
00:12:25,740 --> 00:12:30,080
Katie, my girl, you scared me to death.

198
00:12:31,180 --> 00:12:33,020
Well, you know what I mean.

199
00:12:35,700 --> 00:12:40,840
All I really want to know, Kate, Is that you're going to be all right?

200
00:12:44,520 --> 00:12:47,240
You are going to be all right, aren't you, Kate?

201
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:55,760
Huh? Mike! Kate, I want to show you something I found.

202
00:12:56,040 --> 00:12:57,740
What's that? The first report card you forged?

203
00:12:57,980 --> 00:13:02,860
No. No, it's a love letter from my grandfather to my grandmother.

204
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:06,160
Grandpa Ed wrote this when he was stationed in the Pacific in World War II.

205
00:13:06,540 --> 00:13:09,080
Oh? Now, just listen.

206
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:17,580
My dearest Katie, here I am on this godforsaken island, and all I can think about is

207
00:13:17,580 --> 00:13:20,440
you. I don't deserve you.

208
00:13:20,620 --> 00:13:22,800
I'm vain, selfish, and immature.

209
00:13:23,440 --> 00:13:26,340
But out here with the bullets flying, I'm growing up fast.

210
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:28,400
I love you, Kate.

211
00:13:29,260 --> 00:13:34,060
And I pray the Lord keeps me safe so I can return home to your arms.

212
00:13:34,060 --> 00:13:36,580
Your devoted soldier boy,

213
00:13:37,460 --> 00:13:40,860
Eddie. Oh, Mike, that's beautiful.

214
00:13:42,740 --> 00:13:47,360
Kate, this letter says what I've been trying to say to you better than I ever could.

215
00:13:48,120 --> 00:13:52,040
Oh, Mike. Oh, Kate.

216
00:13:52,700 --> 00:13:59,940
Oh, please! A quiet place to sleep, that's all I ask.

217
00:14:04,680 --> 00:14:07,560
Your son just shared the most wonderful thing with me.

218
00:14:07,860 --> 00:14:09,960
It's a love letter from your dad to your mom.

219
00:14:10,180 --> 00:14:11,840
Oh, not that darn thing again.

220
00:14:12,560 --> 00:14:14,300
Love letter? Let me see that.

221
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:16,500
Oh, boy. It's so beautiful.

222
00:14:16,780 --> 00:14:20,400
Especially the part about growing up fast when the bullets are flying.

223
00:14:21,060 --> 00:14:22,400
Ha ha ha ha ha ha.

224
00:14:22,940 --> 00:14:23,880
What's so funny?

225
00:14:24,420 --> 00:14:27,420
This letter. It's a crock.

226
00:14:28,620 --> 00:14:29,180
Ha ha ha ha.

227
00:14:30,900 --> 00:14:31,980
What do you mean?

228
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:33,280
Grandpa Ed didn't write it?

229
00:14:33,280 --> 00:14:37,000
No, he wrote it, but not from some godforsaken island in the Pacific.

230
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:39,100
He wrote it from the mess hall at Fort Dix.

231
00:14:39,380 --> 00:14:41,580
The only thing flying was cream -chip beef.

232
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:45,420
He never shipped out.

233
00:14:45,580 --> 00:14:48,500
He mailed this an hour before the war ended.

234
00:14:48,860 --> 00:14:51,720
Hey, was it my fault that peace broke out right after lunch?

235
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:54,680
Does Mike know about all of this?

236
00:14:54,900 --> 00:14:55,820
Well, he should.

237
00:14:56,020 --> 00:14:58,280
It's been a family joke for years.

238
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:02,440
Excuse me, I have to get some wood for the fire.

239
00:15:03,280 --> 00:15:05,400
I gotta chop something now.

240
00:15:07,860 --> 00:15:08,740
Oh, Mom, good.

241
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:11,300
I've been looking for that pile of clothes we left on the couch.

242
00:15:11,300 --> 00:15:12,220
Do you know what happened to them?

243
00:15:12,300 --> 00:15:13,200
What pile of clothes?

244
00:15:13,720 --> 00:15:16,640
The ones we spent all day packing and putting in boxes.

245
00:15:16,880 --> 00:15:18,580
Put them all back in the closet.

246
00:15:19,080 --> 00:15:20,080
You did what?

247
00:15:20,780 --> 00:15:24,960
You said you needed boxes for some clothes for the homeless.

248
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:29,100
Mom, those were the clothes for the homeless.

249
00:15:29,660 --> 00:15:32,660
Then what are they doing back in the closet?

250
00:15:33,460 --> 00:15:36,020
Father, where is your head?

251
00:15:36,200 --> 00:15:38,200
Watch that tone with your mother.

252
00:15:38,920 --> 00:15:40,180
Where is your head?

253
00:15:41,580 --> 00:15:46,140
I'm sorry. I don't know what's the matter with me lately.

254
00:15:46,140 --> 00:15:47,820
No, no, Mom, I'm sorry I yelled at you.

255
00:15:47,880 --> 00:15:49,920
I'm just, I'm just worried about you.

256
00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:51,840
Well, I'll get the clothes.

257
00:15:53,640 --> 00:15:57,060
You used to walk away from me when I yelled at you, Kate.

258
00:15:58,400 --> 00:16:00,900
So that's what your face looked like, huh?

259
00:16:02,540 --> 00:16:03,600
What was that all about?

260
00:16:03,700 --> 00:16:05,000
I could hear the yelling in there.

261
00:16:05,520 --> 00:16:07,520
Jason, I don't know what I'm gonna do with my mom.

262
00:16:08,100 --> 00:16:11,180
She says she wants to move to Florida, but by the way she's acting, you'd never know

263
00:16:11,180 --> 00:16:14,440
it. Well, when my patients behave like that, I look at what they're doing, not what

264
00:16:14,440 --> 00:16:16,600
they're saying. Oh, horse hockey.

265
00:16:17,560 --> 00:16:20,140
I've seen better wisdom than broadening on a couch pillow.

266
00:16:21,800 --> 00:16:23,800
Hey, Luke, you want to help me stack some cinder blocks?

267
00:16:24,120 --> 00:16:25,260
Oh, yeah, sure thing.

268
00:16:25,560 --> 00:16:26,820
I'll go get the medicine kit.

269
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:37,440
I swear I didn't know.

270
00:16:37,860 --> 00:16:39,560
Why should I believe anything you say?

271
00:16:40,160 --> 00:16:41,860
Because you're still armed.

272
00:16:43,960 --> 00:16:46,620
Oh, uh, Grandma, excuse me.

273
00:16:46,620 --> 00:16:47,800
I've got to go wash up.

274
00:16:48,100 --> 00:16:49,920
He was always a messy eater.

275
00:16:51,460 --> 00:16:54,080
Your grandson can be so exasperating.

276
00:16:54,320 --> 00:16:56,100
It's his deviated septum.

277
00:16:57,960 --> 00:17:01,800
No. We were fighting about this.

278
00:17:02,900 --> 00:17:06,800
Not again. I'm the ghost here and this thing keeps haunting me.

279
00:17:08,320 --> 00:17:10,780
I haven't seen this in years.

280
00:17:10,840 --> 00:17:11,820
Oh, here it comes.

281
00:17:12,160 --> 00:17:14,800
She's never forgiven me for writing that stupid letter.

282
00:17:15,580 --> 00:17:16,460
Oh, I'm sorry.

283
00:17:16,520 --> 00:17:17,780
I shouldn't have shown that to you.

284
00:17:17,820 --> 00:17:19,240
I'm sure it's a sore spot.

285
00:17:19,640 --> 00:17:22,300
Sore spot? Hell, it almost ended our engagement.

286
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:24,420
I'll let you in on a secret.

287
00:17:24,700 --> 00:17:27,720
What's that? I married Ed because of this letter.

288
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:36,460
Huh? Oh, true, he stretched the truth a bit, but it was the first time he said he

289
00:17:36,460 --> 00:17:41,580
loved me. Granted, it took a world war to get him to say it.

290
00:17:42,320 --> 00:17:50,460
But with men like Ed, you've got to look beyond the blarney and listen to what your

291
00:17:50,460 --> 00:17:52,500
heart is saying.

292
00:17:54,880 --> 00:17:57,360
Just thinking about it gives me goosebumps.

293
00:18:00,340 --> 00:18:01,500
You're not the only one.

294
00:18:06,420 --> 00:18:08,540
I think we're gonna look for Mike.

295
00:18:10,760 --> 00:18:13,000
Ow, ow, ow!

296
00:18:15,080 --> 00:18:18,740
What happened? Jason dropped a cinder block on his toe.

297
00:18:18,880 --> 00:18:19,900
I didn't drop it.

298
00:18:19,900 --> 00:18:20,660
I didn't drop it.

299
00:18:20,660 --> 00:18:23,480
There was a gust of wind right between his ears.

300
00:18:25,700 --> 00:18:27,120
Jason, your toe will be fine.

301
00:18:27,220 --> 00:18:28,620
Just let me put a bandage on it.

302
00:18:28,640 --> 00:18:29,840
I can bandage it myself.

303
00:18:30,020 --> 00:18:31,840
Maggie, you're treating me like I'm in an accident.

304
00:18:31,960 --> 00:18:32,400
What the hell?

305
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:36,740
I was gonna replace that window anyway.

306
00:18:37,660 --> 00:18:38,700
Yeah, come on, see?

307
00:18:38,980 --> 00:18:39,760
Watch the toe.

308
00:18:40,060 --> 00:18:40,940
Watch the toe.

309
00:18:41,060 --> 00:18:42,860
I am. Can I have a glass of water?

310
00:18:43,740 --> 00:18:54,520
Oh, sure. Okay, forget the glass.

311
00:18:56,040 --> 00:19:00,540
Uh, sorry. Oh, I'm glad I'm leaving.

312
00:19:00,700 --> 00:19:02,520
I can't handle this house anymore.

313
00:19:03,040 --> 00:19:04,660
Why? This is a great house.

314
00:19:04,940 --> 00:19:07,180
Don't you let a little broken washer chase you out.

315
00:19:07,260 --> 00:19:09,020
Oh, the washer's broken, too.

316
00:19:09,080 --> 00:19:11,240
No, I mean the washer and the faucet.

317
00:19:11,540 --> 00:19:12,440
It's easy to fix.

318
00:19:13,260 --> 00:19:14,220
Not for me.

319
00:19:14,680 --> 00:19:19,360
Ed always said if there was a way to break a machine, I'd find it.

320
00:19:20,980 --> 00:19:24,740
He, uh... He made me feel so helpless at times.

321
00:19:26,220 --> 00:19:30,160
Oh, Kate. I never wanted you to feel that way.

322
00:19:30,160 --> 00:19:31,920
I mean, I was just a little impatient.

323
00:19:32,100 --> 00:19:35,700
That's all. Oh, good Lord.

324
00:19:36,620 --> 00:19:38,620
Now I know why they sent me back.

325
00:19:38,680 --> 00:19:41,420
They wanted me to see what I turned you into.

326
00:19:43,260 --> 00:19:46,620
The gentlest, sweetest woman I know.

327
00:19:48,220 --> 00:19:50,360
I've made a frame of the world.

328
00:19:51,280 --> 00:19:53,700
Grandma Kate, believe me, changing a washer is a snap.

329
00:19:53,940 --> 00:19:54,840
You just don't let Dr.

330
00:19:55,100 --> 00:19:55,860
Siever help you.

331
00:19:56,440 --> 00:19:56,840
Now, come here.

332
00:19:56,840 --> 00:19:57,920
I'll show you how to do it.

333
00:19:59,480 --> 00:20:04,880
Come on. Now, here's where the water enters the house,

334
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:08,120
okay? Take this.

335
00:20:11,160 --> 00:20:15,820
Now, all you have to do is turn this clockwise.

336
00:20:15,820 --> 00:20:18,780
Oh, I don't know.

337
00:20:19,180 --> 00:20:21,420
Come on, girl, you can do it.

338
00:20:22,320 --> 00:20:24,480
Clockwise? Clockwise.

339
00:20:41,740 --> 00:20:44,240
And now, the test.

340
00:20:50,300 --> 00:20:53,480
Mrs. Maloney just single -handedly turned off the water.

341
00:20:53,520 --> 00:20:55,580
Oh, no. Oh, no, no.

342
00:20:55,580 --> 00:20:58,420
That's good. Oh, boy!

343
00:20:58,880 --> 00:21:00,060
I did it!

344
00:21:00,160 --> 00:21:01,860
I know you could do it.

345
00:21:02,040 --> 00:21:05,900
Now, let me out that washer -doo -hickey.

346
00:21:08,660 --> 00:21:10,220
Let's go, everybody!

347
00:21:10,440 --> 00:21:12,900
Last one to the table is a rotten apple.

348
00:21:13,160 --> 00:21:15,180
Egg, Kate. A rotten egg?

349
00:21:15,180 --> 00:21:17,040
Well, I guess Apple would work.

350
00:21:18,180 --> 00:21:20,160
Mom, you unpacked everything.

351
00:21:20,220 --> 00:21:21,960
What's going on with you?

352
00:21:22,260 --> 00:21:24,960
If everyone will sit down, I have an announcement.

353
00:21:25,980 --> 00:21:28,080
I fixed the leaky faucet.

354
00:21:30,420 --> 00:21:31,900
Now, let's eat.

355
00:21:32,860 --> 00:21:34,560
Uh, Mom, that's your announcement?

356
00:21:34,820 --> 00:21:35,620
Uh, uh -huh.

357
00:21:35,940 --> 00:21:40,240
Oh, uh... And I'm not moving to Florida.

358
00:21:40,580 --> 00:21:43,000
You're not? Mom, that's great.

359
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:44,640
But, Mom, you have to.

360
00:21:44,840 --> 00:21:47,220
You said yourself that this house is too much for you to handle.

361
00:21:47,380 --> 00:21:49,860
No. Everyone else said it.

362
00:21:50,300 --> 00:21:53,200
The problem was, I believed it.

363
00:21:53,820 --> 00:21:55,320
I want to stay.

364
00:21:56,120 --> 00:21:59,100
There's a lifetime of memories here.

365
00:22:00,520 --> 00:22:02,000
You called them ghosts.

366
00:22:02,820 --> 00:22:04,700
I like the ghosts.

367
00:22:06,980 --> 00:22:08,940
I'm glad you're staying, Grandma.

368
00:22:09,340 --> 00:22:11,060
Oh, Mom, so am I.

369
00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:13,140
Good girl, Katie.

370
00:22:13,480 --> 00:22:15,160
Well, my work here is done.

371
00:22:16,220 --> 00:22:19,460
Gee, maybe if I hurry, I can catch up with Sophocles on the back nine.

372
00:22:20,560 --> 00:22:22,000
So what's for lunch, Grandma?

373
00:22:22,580 --> 00:22:26,360
Tuna casserole. It was Ed's favorite.

374
00:22:27,500 --> 00:22:30,860
Oh, that's the one with the potato chips crumbled on the top.

375
00:22:31,160 --> 00:22:32,760
It's a pity I can't eat anymore.

376
00:22:35,260 --> 00:22:40,240
Oh. Oh, heck, I'll just wait for the master's turtle.

377
00:22:42,140 --> 00:22:43,140
Whip Michelangelo's butt.

378
00:22:45,660 --> 00:22:46,880
Yeah, have some, uh...

379
00:22:47,720 --> 00:22:50,680
Thank you. We have come to meet you.

380
00:22:50,700 --> 00:22:51,900
Can you give Chrissy some castor oil?

381
00:22:53,340 --> 00:22:54,620
Yeah. Take you to Bigg's.

382
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:55,460
It's a great show.

383
00:22:59,300 --> 00:23:33,920
Yeah. Thank you.
